WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.900
How does greedy coin change

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.580
work, and when does it

3
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.520
fail? We will make change

4
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.100
with a locally best choice,

5
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.020
count the work, and then

6
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.020
use one counterexample to test

7
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.640
whether that choice is always

8
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.200
optimal.

9
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.020
A greedy algorithm commits to

10
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.580
its next choice using the

11
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.240
information currently available. It does

12
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.640
not systematically compare all future

13
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.620
continuations or undo the choice

14
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:25.920
when a later step becomes

15
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.400
awkward.

16
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.500
For coin change, the natural

17
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.760
rule is to select the

18
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.100
largest coin that does not

19
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.520
exceed the remaining amount. Repeat

20
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.080
until the remainder is zero,

21
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.880
assuming positive integer denominations and

22
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.520
an unlimited supply of coins.

23
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.460
Take denominations one, five, ten

24
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.900
and twenty-five, and make thirty-seven.

25
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.060
The largest affordable coin is

26
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.560
twenty-five, leaving twelve; move that

27
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.200
coin into the selected row

28
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.760
rather than treating it as

29
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.840
a mathematical wish.

30
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.360
For the remainder twelve, the

31
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.360
largest affordable coin is ten.

32
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.000
Selecting it leaves two, which

33
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.740
neither five nor ten can

34
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.280
fill exactly by itself.

35
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.960
Take a one, leaving one,

36
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.280
then a final one, leaving

37
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.080
zero. The completed change is

38
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.480
twenty-five plus ten plus one

39
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.760
plus one, using four coins.

40
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.720
A faster implementation handles one

41
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.260
denomination at a time in

42
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.880
descending order. Integer division tells

43
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.280
how many copies to take,

44
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.140
and the remainder moves directly

45
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.220
to the next denomination without

46
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.180
an iteration for every individual

47
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:32.600
coin.

48
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.120
If k denominations are already

49
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.500
ordered, this division-based scan uses

50
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.000
big O of k arithmetic

51
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.900
operations under the course word

52
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.180
model. Printing the chosen coins

53
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.480
separately adds output work, while

54
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.260
an unsorted input may also

55
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.120
require sorting.

56
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.560
The repeated-subtraction version instead performs

57
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.500
one choice for every coin

58
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.760
selected. Its cost depends on

59
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.380
how the largest affordable coin

60
00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.740
is found; rescanning all k

61
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.680
denominations each time adds a

62
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.460
factor that a constant-denomination assumption

63
00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:05.020
would hide.

64
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.500
Now change the denominations to

65
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.740
one, ten and twenty-five, and

66
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.400
request thirty. Greedy still takes

67
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.860
twenty-five first, but the remainder

68
00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.700
five requires five separate one-coins,

69
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.820
giving six coins in total.

70
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.740
Three ten-coins also make thirty,

71
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.680
and use only three coins.

72
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.300
This single strictly better feasible

73
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.300
solution is enough to disprove

74
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.640
universal optimality of largest-first greedy

75
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.860
on arbitrary denomination systems.

76
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.600
Having a one-coin ensures every

77
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.700
nonnegative integer amount is reachable,

78
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.420
but it says nothing about

79
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.960
minimum coin count. Some denomination

80
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.800
systems do admit this greedy

81
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.940
rule, whereas proving that property

82
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.480
requires more than testing a

83
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:50.440
few convenient amounts.

84
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.360
Things to take note of:

85
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.500
state the objective, denomination assumptions,

86
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.700
choice rule and actual implementation

87
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.460
cost. A counterexample disproves a

88
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.300
general greedy claim, while many

89
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.460
successful traces cannot establish it;

90
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.540
when the choice fails, dynamic

91
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.620
programming can compare the alternatives,

92
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.700
hmph.
